    title: 'Without Inherent Entity, No Inherent Non-Entity',
    sanskrit: { text: '', transliteration: '', translation: 'If the entity is not established, a nonentity is not established. An entity that has become different is a nonentity, people say.' },
    philosophy: {
      insight: 'The cascade completes: without inherent entity (bhāva), there\'s no inherent non-entity (abhāva). Non-entity is just entity-that-changed — "people say." Both entity and non-entity are conventional designations, not inherent realities. The entire four-fold framework (essence, otherness, entity, non-entity) is empty.',
      madhyamaka: 'Abhāva (non-entity) is defined as the alteration or cessation of bhāva (entity). If bhāva isn\'t inherently established (v.4), abhāva can\'t be either — it\'s defined relative to bhāva. "People say" (loka) that what was X and became different is "non-X." This is a conventional designation, not an inherent fact.',
      quantum: 'Particle-antiparticle creation and annihilation: particles appear from and vanish into the quantum vacuum. Neither "existence" nor "non-existence" of a particle is inherent — both are states of the quantum field. The field persists; the "entity" and "non-entity" are field excitations/de-excitations.',
      bridge: 'Both pair creation/annihilation and Mādhyamika show entity/non-entity are relative states, not inherent realities. QM: particle → vacuum → particle is a field fluctuation. Mādhyamika: entity → non-entity is a conventional description of change. Neither "entity" nor "non-entity" is inherent.',
      accessible: 'If there\'s no inherent "entity" (v.4), there can\'t be an inherent "non-entity" either. "Non-entity" is just what people CALL an entity that changed. Like a melted ice cube isn\'t a "non-ice-cube" — it\'s water. The "non-entity" label is conventional, not inherent.',
      twoTruths: 'Conventionally, things exist and cease. Ultimately, neither inherent entity nor inherent non-entity can be established. Both labels are conventional designations, not inherent realities.',
      commonMisconception: 'NOT saying nothing exists or that existence/non-existence are meaningless. Both work conventionally. The point: neither is INHERENT. An entity doesn\'t inherently exist, and a non-entity doesn\'t inherently not-exist.',
    },
